{"id": 4024222, "title": "CVE-2026-92391: curl_mime_free stack overflow by recursion", "submitted": "2026-09-12", "source_url": "https://hackerone.com/reports/4024222", "excerpt": "Hi all,\n\nI've discovered a stack overflow in libcurl's MIME cleanup code. The read path in the MIME implementation is protected by a recursion depth limit (`MAX_MIME_LEVELS = 40`, defined at line 47 of `lib/mime.c` and checked in `read_part_content` and related functions), but the cleanup path has no equivalent guard. A deeply nested multipart structure built through the public API will crash the process when freed.\n\n## Impact\n\nAny application using the libcurl MIME API (`curl_mime_addpart` / `curl_mime_subparts`) to process user-controlled MIME structure depth is at risk. The crash happens inside `curl_mime_free` as part of normal cleanup, so once the structure is built, there is no way to avoid it. In a server-side context where an application constructs a MIME request based on external input, an attacker can cause reliable process termination by supplying input that drives the nesting depth high enough. This does not appear to be exploitable for code execution.\n\n## Root cause\n\nThe cleanup functions form a mutual recursion with no depth limit. When a `curl_mimepart` holds subparts (set via `curl_mime_subparts`), the part's `freefunc` is set to `mime_subparts_free`. During `curl_mime_free`, the call chain is:\n\n`curl_mime_free` -> `Curl_mime_cleanpart` -> `cleanup_part_content` -> `part->freefunc()` (i.e., `mime_subparts_free`) -> `curl_mime_free` -> ...\n\nThe relevant code in `lib/mime.c`:\n\n```c\n/* line 1056 */\nstatic void cleanup_part_content(curl_mimepart *part)\n{\n  if(part->freefunc)\n    part->freefunc(part->arg);   /* calls mime_subparts_free for multipart parts */\n  ...\n}\n\n/* line 1075 */\nstatic void mime_subparts_free(void *ptr)\n{\n  curl_mime *mime = (curl_mime *)ptr;\n\n  if(mime && mime->parent) {\n    mime->parent->freefunc = NULL;\n    cleanup_part_content(mime->parent);\n  }\n  curl_mime_free(mime);          /* recurses back into curl_mime_free */\n}\n\n/* line 1113 */\nvoid curl_mime_free(curl_mime *mime)\n{\n  curl_mimepart *part;\n\n  if(mime) {\n    mime_subparts_unbind(mime);\n    while(mime->firstpart) {\n      part = mime->firstpart;\n      mime->firstpart = part->nextpart;\n      Curl_mime_cleanpart(part);   /* calls cleanup_part_content */\n      curlx_free(part);\n    }\n    curlx_free(mime);\n  }\n}\n```\n\nEach nesting level adds roughly four stack frames. At 5000 levels the stack overflows (just an example number here, obvious dependent on system, memory, the o", "excerpt_truncated": true, "final": "likely_valid", "final_label": "Confirmed by the code", "tone": "good", "explain": "The real code shows the flaw the report describes.", "confidence": 0.95, "judge_verdict": "likely_valid", "receipts_override": "", "policy_section": "", "grounding": {"verdict": "partially_grounded", "rev": "7bbaf2fffd94", "rev_source": "master on 2026-09-12", "counts": {"grounded": 20, "ungrounded_strong": 0, "ungrounded_medium": 1}, "problems": [{"check": "line", "status": "ungrounded", "severity": "medium", "claim": "lib/mime.c:47", "evidence": "none of cleanup_part_content, freefunc, mime_subparts_free, curl_mime_free appear near line 47 at 7bbaf2fffd94; it reads: '#define MAX_MIME_LEVELS 40 /* avoid deep nesting */'"}]}, "summary": "A stack overflow occurs in libcurl's MIME cleanup code due to unbounded recursion when freeing deeply nested multipart structures.", "claims": [{"id": "C1", "kind": "flaw", "text": "The cleanup functions form a mutual recursion with no depth limit, causing stack overflow during MIME structure cleanup.", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "The code shows a clear mutual recursion chain: curl_mime_free -> Curl_mime_cleanpart -> cleanup_part_content -> mime_subparts_free -> curl_mime_free, with no depth limit in any of these functions. The read path (read_part_content) has a MAX_MIME_LEVELS=40 guard, but the cleanup path lacks any equivalent protection.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/mime.c", "line": 1059, "note": "cleanup_part_content calls part->freefunc(part->arg) with no depth check", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/mime.c", "line": 1081, "note": "mime_subparts_free calls cleanup_part_content(mime->parent)", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/mime.c", "line": 1083, "note": "mime_subparts_free calls curl_mime_free(mime) - mutual recursion", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/mime.c", "line": 1122, "note": "curl_mime_free calls Curl_mime_cleanpart(part)", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/mime.c", "line": 1101, "note": "Curl_mime_cleanpart calls cleanup_part_content(part)", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/mime.c", "line": 709, "note": "read_part_content has depth check: if(++call_depth > MAX_MIME_LEVELS) return READ_ERROR;", "valid": true}]}, {"id": "C2", "kind": "location", "text": "The issue occurs in `curl_mime_free`, `Curl_mime_cleanpart`, `cleanup_part_content`, and `mime_subparts_free` functions in `lib/mime.c`.", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "All four functions cited in the claim exist in lib/mime.c at the shown lines and form the recursive cleanup chain.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/mime.c", "line": 1113, "note": "curl_mime_free definition", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/mime.c", "line": 1098, "note": "Curl_mime_cleanpart definition", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/mime.c", "line": 1056, "note": "cleanup_part_content definition", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/mime.c", "line": 1075, "note": "mime_subparts_free definition", "valid": true}]}, {"id": "C3", "kind": "trigger", "text": "A deeply nested multipart structure built via `curl_mime_addpart` and `curl_mime_subparts` triggers the stack overflow when freed.", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "The construction APIs (curl_mime_addpart, curl_mime_subparts) impose no nesting depth limit. When a part's kind is MIMEKIND_MULTIPART, its freefunc is set to mime_subparts_free, which triggers the recursive cleanup chain when curl_mime_free is called on the root.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/mime.c", "line": 1244, "note": "curl_mime_addpart creates parts with no depth limit", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/mime.c", "line": 1519, "note": "curl_mime_subparts sets subparts with no depth limit", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/mime.c", "line": 1075, "note": "mime_subparts_free is set as freefunc for multipart parts", "valid": true}]}, {"id": "C4", "kind": "impact", "text": "Process termination via stack overflow when freeing the MIME structure.", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "The mutual recursion has no termination condition based on depth. Each level adds ~4 stack frames (per report, consistent with call chain). At sufficient depth (e.g., 5000 in PoC), this exhausts the stack, causing process termination (stack overflow).", "citations": [{"file": "lib/mime.c", "line": 1059, "note": "Recursive call chain with no base case for depth", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/mime.c", "line": 1083, "note": "Each nesting level adds multiple stack frames", "valid": true}]}, {"id": "C5", "kind": "reproduction", "text": "The provided proof of concept constructs 5000 nested MIME levels and calls `curl_mime_free` to trigger the overflow.", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "The PoC logic maps directly to the API: it creates 5000 nested MIME levels via curl_mime_addpart and curl_mime_subparts in a loop, then calls curl_mime_free(root). The provided code shows these functions accept unlimited nesting and the cleanup path has no depth guard, so the PoC would trigger the stack overflow as described.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/mime.c", "line": 1244, "note": "curl_mime_addpart used in loop", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/mime.c", "line": 1519, "note": "curl_mime_subparts used in loop", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/mime.c", "line": 1113, "note": "curl_mime_free called on root", "valid": true}]}], "external_checks": [], "external_sources": [{"url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/answers/questions/965596/netstandard-library-1-6-1-source-code", "title": "NETStandard.Library 1.6.1 source code - Microsoft Q&A", "for": "The read path in the MIME implementation is protected by a recursion depth limit (`MAX_MIME_LEVELS = 40`) but the cleanup path has no equivalent guard."}], "key_reasons": ["The cleanup path (curl_mime_free -> Curl_mime_cleanpart -> cleanup_part_content -> mime_subparts_free -> curl_mime_free) forms a mutual recursion with no depth limit, while the read path enforces MAX_MIME_LEVELS=40.", "Construction APIs (curl_mime_addpart, curl_mime_subparts) impose no depth limit, so the cleanup code must handle arbitrarily deep structures.", "The same unbounded recursion exists in multipart_size and Curl_mime_prepare_headers.", "An application processing attacker-controlled MIME structure depth can be crashed reliably via stack overflow during cleanup — a denial-of-service vulnerability in the library."], "missing_info": [], "reply": "Thanks for the detailed report and PoC. The code confirms the cleanup path (curl_mime_free, Curl_mime_cleanpart, cleanup_part_content, mime_subparts_free) recurses mutually with no depth guard, while the read path enforces MAX_MIME_LEVELS=40. Construction APIs also lack a limit. This is a valid denial-of-service issue: an application that builds MIME structures from untrusted input can be crashed on cleanup. We'll add depth tracking to the cleanup, sizing, and header-preparation paths, mirroring the read_part_content pattern.", "code": [{"name": "lib/mime.c", "start": 1113, "end": 1127, "reason": "definition of curl_mime_free", "rows": [{"n": 1113, "text": "void curl_mime_free(curl_mime *mime)", "notes": ["C2: curl_mime_free definition", "C5: curl_mime_free called on root"]}, {"n": 1114, "text": "{", "notes": []}, {"n": 1115, "text": "  curl_mimepart *part;", "notes": []}, {"n": 1116, "text": "", "notes": []}, {"n": 1117, "text": "  if(mime)